# 6. But we need SOURCE CONFIGURATION

This is something I recommend adding now.

Instead of modifying Python every time we want to enable/disable a source, use:

```text
app/config/job_sources.yml
```

Example:

```yaml
sources:

  remoteok:
    enabled: true
    type: remote
    priority: high

  wellfound:
    enabled: true
    type: remote
    priority: high

  ycombinator:
    enabled: true
    type: remote
    priority: high

  indeed:
    enabled: true
    type: job_board
    priority: high

  naukri:
    enabled: true
    type: job_board
    priority: high

  foundit:
    enabled: true
    type: job_board
    priority: high

  shine:
    enabled: true
    type: job_board
    priority: medium

  hirist:
    enabled: true
    type: job_board
    priority: high

  cutshort:
    enabled: true
    type: job_board
    priority: high

  freshersworld:
    enabled: true
    type: job_board
    priority: medium

  company_careers:
    enabled: true
    type: career_page
    priority: high

  recruiter_posts:
    enabled: true
    type: recruiter_post
    priority: high
```

Then:

```text
enabled: true
```

means search it.

```text
enabled: false
```

means skip it.

---

# 7. Daily jobs are NOT the same as recruiter posts

This is very important.

We should have two different concepts.

### Normal job listing

Example:

```text
Company:
ABC Technologies

Role:
Java Developer

Posted:
September 1, 2026

Experience:
0–2 years

Apply:
https://...
```

### Recruiter hiring post

Example:

```text
Hiring Java Developers!

Experience:
0–2 years

Location:
Hyderabad

Send resume:
recruiter@gmail.com
```

The second one may not have a conventional application URL.

Therefore:

```text
JOB CONNECTORS
        |
        v
JOB LISTING

RECRUITER CONNECTORS
        |
        v
HIRING POST
```

Both eventually become a common internal object.

---

# 8. We need a common JobData format

Every connector should return something like:

```text
JobData
```

regardless of where it came from.

For example:

```text
source
source_job_id
title
company
location
remote
employment_type
experience
salary
description
skills
job_url
apply_url
posted_at
recruiter_name
recruiter_email
post_url
source_type
```

Then RemoteOK and Naukri don't need to look identical.

The normalizer converts them into the same structure.

---

# 9. The most important part: TODAY filter

You specifically said:

> if today's report, I want today's hiring jobs

So we need strict freshness handling.

The pipeline should be:

```text
FETCH
  ↓
POSTED DATE EXTRACTION
  ↓
NORMALIZE DATE
  ↓
IS TODAY?
  |
  +---- NO → REJECT
  |
 YES
  ↓
CONTINUE
```

But we also need:

```text
posted today
posted X hours ago
posted today
```

and relative timestamps such as:

```text
2 hours ago
5 hours ago
19 hours ago
```

converted into an actual timestamp.

---

# 10. Don't trust the search-result date alone

This addresses the problem you previously found.

You said:

> report says today's job but opening the link shows 5+ years experience

Exactly.

We need:

```text
SEARCH RESULT
     ↓
GET JOB URL
     ↓
FETCH FULL JOB CONTENT
     ↓
PARSE FULL DESCRIPTION
     ↓
EXTRACT EXPERIENCE
     ↓
EXTRACT ROLE
     ↓
EXTRACT SKILLS
     ↓
VALIDATE
```

The search result is only **discovery**.

The actual job page is the **source of truth**, when legally/technically accessible.

---

# 11. Experience extraction needs to be much stronger

We shouldn't simply search for:

```text
"2 years"
```

We need an `ExperienceAnalyzer`.

It should understand:

```text
0-2 years
0 to 2 years
1-2 years
1+ years
2 years
freshers
fresher
entry level
graduate
junior
experienced
2-4 years
3-5 years
5+ years
minimum 3 years
```

And detect things like:

```text
Experience: 0–2 years
```

as:

```text
minimum = 0
maximum = 2
```

---

# 12. Your filter

For your current target:

```text
MAX_EXPERIENCE = 2
```

So:

```text
0 years     → PASS
Fresher     → PASS
0–1         → PASS
0–2         → PASS
1–2         → PASS
2 years     → PASS
2–3         → REVIEW/REJECT depending on policy
3+          → REJECT
5+          → REJECT
8+          → REJECT
```

And this should happen **before resume tailoring**.

That saves time and API cost.

---

# 13. Role filter

Then:

```text
Experience PASS
       ↓
Role extraction
       ↓
Career Profile
       ↓
Role similarity
```

For example:

```text
Java Developer
Java Full Stack Developer
Spring Boot Developer
Backend Developer
Software Engineer
Full Stack Developer
Microservices Developer
```

are relevant.

But:

```text
HR Recruiter
US Staffing Recruiter
Sales Executive
Business Development Executive
```

are not.

---

# 14. Then skill matching

For example:

```text
JD:

Java
Spring Boot
REST API
Microservices
SQL
AWS
React
```

Compare against your resume:

```text
Java
Spring Boot
Microservices
REST
SQL
AWS
React
```

Then:

```text
skill_score = 88
```

---

# 15. Final score

We can eventually use:

```text
Overall Score =
    30% Role Match
  + 25% Experience Match
  + 25% Skill Match
  + 10% Location/Remote Match
  + 10% Freshness/Source Quality
```

For example:

```text
Java Full Stack Developer
0–2 years
Remote
Java + Spring Boot + React + AWS
Posted today

Score = 94%
```

---

# 16. Then resume tailoring

Only after:

```text
TODAY
+
EXPERIENCE
+
ROLE
+
SKILLS
+
SCORE
```

passes.

Then:

```text
JD
 ↓
Resume
 ↓
Claude/AI
 ↓
Tailored Resume
 ↓
PDF
```

Again, the agent must **never invent experience, projects, employers, certifications, or skills** just to increase the score.

